<Chapter id="metadata">
<h2>Embedded BSN schema metadata</h2>
  <p>A Sunken service route selects the payload schema. The payload schema sets the
  service-record boundary. A service-less command response uses its fixed 16-bit
  layout.</p>
  <p>SC2 embeds compact version-7 BSN metadata in the client executable. Source-path
  strings in the documented client identify the producer as <code>bsnc</code>
  version <code>2.2.13</code>.</p>
  <p><code>bsnc</code> is Blizzard's schema compiler and serialization runtime for
  Sunken's bit-packed protocol. It turns protocol definitions into native encoders,
  decoders, size calculators, and compact metadata tables. Those tables describe
  type kinds, member references, names, integer and count widths, accepted ranges,
  and control flags. Generated readers, writers, and layout sidecars supply field
  permutations and filler runs for selected types.</p>
  <p>A normal client can use a generated static schema. Inspection tools can load a
  metadata file or extract the largest metadata block from an executable.</p>

  <h3 id="metadata-resolution">Schema lookup</h3>
  <MetadataResolutionDiagram />

  <p>Each metadata type has a build-specific numeric ID. The ID selects a structural
  record. It can also select a naming record. A structural record can refer to
  other type IDs. Thus, one payload type produces a type graph.</p>

  <p>The definitions in this document summarize the decoded type graph. They show
  values and relationships. Separate wire-layout data supplies generated field
  order and filler runs.</p>
  <p>The metadata block contains a dump-offset table and a name-offset table. A
  type ID is the zero-based position used to read both tables. For the documented
  client build, type ID <code>351</code> selects the structural record and name
  record for <code>Battlenet::Authentication::ModuleRequest</code>.</p>
  <p>The decoded definition of that record is an array whose count occupies three
  bits, whose accepted range is <code>0..=4</code>, and whose element type resolves
  to <code>ModuleInput</code>. <code>Authentication::ProofRequest</code> reaches it
  through the following metadata entries:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Type</th><th>Declaration</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ProofRequest</code> (record <code>1422</code>)</td>
        <td>Member <code>m_request</code> resolves through alias <code>1424</code> to type <code>351</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ModuleRequest</code> (record <code>351</code>)</td>
        <td>Kind <code>array</code>; 3-bit count; accepted range <code>0..=4</code>; element alias <code>352</code> resolves to record <code>346</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ModuleInput</code> (record <code>346</code>)</td>
        <td>Struct containing <code>m_id</code> and <code>m_data</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ModuleId</code> (record <code>344</code>)</td>
        <td>Alias resolving to a fixed 40-byte blob.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ModuleData</code> (record <code>345</code>)</td>
        <td>Kind <code>blob</code>; 10-bit byte count; accepted range <code>0..=1023</code>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>These IDs remain in the decoder's schema lookup. Applying the resolved types
  produces this Sunken record layout:</p>
  <RustSchema code={`record AuthProofRequest {
    command_id: Bits<6> = 2,
    service_present: Bits<1> = 1,
    service_slot: Bits<4> = 0,
    payload: ProofRequest,
    final_alignment: byte,
}

struct ProofRequest {
    requests: ModuleRequest,
}

type ModuleRequest = Array<ModuleInput> {
    count_bits: 3,
    count_range: 0..=4,
}

struct ModuleInput {
    id: ModuleId,
    data: ModuleData,
}

type ModuleData = Blob {
    byte_count_bits: 10,
    byte_count_range: 0..=1023,
    alignment: byte,
}`} />
  <p><code>requests</code> is the logical field represented by
  <code>request_count</code> and the repeated values. Three bits can represent
  <code>0..=7</code>; the declared range rejects <code>5..=7</code>.</p>

  <h3 id="types">Type kinds</h3>
  <p>The low six bits of a structural record identify one of 16 kinds. The kind
  defines the remaining metadata. The kind also defines how the value consumes the
  Sunken bitstream.</p>
  <div class="type-vocabulary">
    <section>
      <h4>Scalar values</h4>
      <dl>
        <dt><code>5</code> Boolean</dt><dd>One bit.</dd>
        <dt><code>7</code> enum</dt><dd>A bounded integer, optionally restricted to listed values.</dd>
        <dt><code>8</code> FourCC</dt><dd>Exactly 32 bits.</dd>
        <dt><code>9</code> integer</dt><dd>A bounded, fixed-width integer.</dd>
        <dt><code>10</code> void</dt><dd>Zero bits.</dd>
        <dt><code>12</code> float32</dt><dd>32 raw bits.</dd>
        <dt><code>13</code> float64</dt><dd>64 raw bits.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h4>Containers</h4>
      <dl>
        <dt><code>1</code> array</dt><dd>Metadata-sized element count, then that many values of another type.</dd>
        <dt><code>2</code> byte string</dt><dd>Metadata-sized byte count, byte alignment, then that many raw bytes.</dd>
        <dt><code>3</code> bit array</dt><dd>Metadata-sized bit count, then that many packed bits.</dd>
        <dt><code>4</code> blob</dt><dd>Metadata-sized byte count, byte alignment, then that many raw bytes.</dd>
        <dt><code>11</code> optional</dt><dd>One presence bit, then the contained value when present.</dd>
        <dt><code>15</code> UTF-8 string</dt><dd>Metadata-sized byte count, alignment, then that many UTF-8 bytes.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h4>Composition</h4>
      <dl>
        <dt><code>6</code> choice</dt><dd>A bounded selector followed by the selected arm.</dd>
        <dt><code>14</code> struct</dt><dd>A sequence of named or unnamed member types.</dd>
        <dt><code>16</code> alias</dt><dd>A transparent reference to another type.</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>
  </div>
  <p>Most bounded values encode <code>value - minimum</code>. Metadata supplies the
  bit width. A fixed-size value can omit the count. For some indexed types, bit 7
  identifies a continuous index range.</p>

  <h3 id="metadata-container">Metadata storage</h3>
  <p>Use this binary layout to extract BSN metadata from an SC2 build. Network
  records contain values selected by the extracted schemas.</p>

  <div class="metadata-container" aria-label="Five consecutive regions in a version seven BSN metadata block">
    <div><strong>Header</strong><span>5 × big-endian <code>u32</code></span></div>
    <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><strong>Shape offsets</strong><span>one <code>u32</code> per type ID</span></div>
    <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><strong>Shape records</strong><span><code>dump_size</code> bytes</span></div>
    <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><strong>Name offsets</strong><span>one <code>u32</code> per type ID</span></div>
    <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
    <div><strong>Name records</strong><span><code>string_size</code> bytes</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>Type ID <code>n</code> selects element <code>n</code> in both offset tables. The
  shape offset points to the structural record. The name offset points to a naming
  record. A zero name offset identifies an unnamed type.</p>
  <p>The naming record contains 32-bit offsets into a NUL-terminated UTF-8 string
  pool. The <code>type_count</code>, <code>dump_size</code>, and
  <code>string_size</code> fields set the five region boundaries.</p>

  <RustSchema code={`struct MetadataHeader {
    version: BeU32, // currently 7
    flags: BeU32,
    type_count: BeU32,
    dump_size: BeU32,
    string_size: BeU32,
}

struct MetadataBlock {
    header: MetadataHeader,
    dump_offsets: Vec<BeU32>,          // length = type_count
    dump_data: Vec<u8>,                // length = dump_size
    string_record_offsets: Vec<BeU32>, // length = type_count
    string_data: Vec<u8>,              // length = string_size
}`} />

  <p>The first byte of each structural record begins with:</p>
<pre is:raw><span class="syn-field">bits 0..5</span>  kind tag (<span class="syn-number">1..16</span>)
<span class="syn-field">bit  6</span>     generated-handling marker
<span class="syn-field">bit  7</span>     kind-specific compact-form flag
</pre>
  <p>The selected kind defines bit 7. For example, bit 7 can identify an implicit
  continuous index range.</p>
  <BackToContents />
</Chapter>
